WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.470
Many threads create objects at the same time.

2
00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:09.903
All of them go into eden, one area of the heap that every thread shares.

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:15.599
If each new took memory straight from eden, every thread would need this one pointer.

4
00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:16.893
One thread moves it.

5
00:00:16.893 --> 00:00:17.928
The others wait.

6
00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:21.171
How would you stop them fighting over it?

7
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:25.658
Give each thread its own piece of eden.

8
00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:27.383
Now no thread needs a lock.

9
00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:31.935
That piece is a TLAB: a thread-local allocation buffer.

10
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:38.048
To allocate, the thread checks that the object fits before the end.

11
00:00:38.048 --> 00:00:41.276
Then it adds the size to top, and stores the new top.

12
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:43.416
A compare, an add and a store.

13
00:00:43.416 --> 00:00:46.664
The JIT puts them right into your compiled method.

14
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:51.311
What happens when the next object doesn't fit?

15
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.960
The thread takes a fresh TLAB from eden, with one atomic update.

16
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.229
That happens once per TLAB, not once per object.

17
00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:04.306
And sometimes new allocates nothing.

18
00:01:04.306 --> 00:01:08.408
This loop builds a million Points, and only adds up their fields.

19
00:01:08.408 --> 00:01:11.312
In the interpreter, each one is a real object.

20
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:14.507
Can anything outside the method ever see p?

21
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:16.720
No.

22
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:25.153
p never goes into a field or a collection, it's never returned, and it never goes to a call that C2 doesn't inline.

23
00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:29.020
C2, the JIT for hot methods, checks exactly this.

24
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.735
That's escape analysis.

25
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:34.752
Then it skips the object, and keeps x and y in registers.

26
00:01:34.752 --> 00:01:36.493
That's scalar replacement.

27
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.325
Count the bytes of each call.

28
00:01:39.325 --> 00:01:44.571
The first rounds allocate megabytes, while the interpreter and C1 run the code.

29
00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:46.231
From round 4 or so, zero.

30
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:53.340
With escape analysis off, every round allocates 32,000,000 bytes: a million 32-byte points.

31
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:57.371
So never assert that code allocates nothing in a unit test.

32
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.544
Java and Kotlin compile to the same new, so both behave the same.

33
00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:11.096
The rule: new is a pointer bump in your own TLAB, and an object that never leaves a hot method may never be allocated.

34
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.178
Quick check.

35
00:02:12.178 --> 00:02:14.737
The loop adds each Point to a list.

36
00:02:14.737 --> 00:02:16.931
Does C2 remove the allocation?

37
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:19.619
No.

38
00:02:19.619 --> 00:02:21.959
The list is a way out.
